@startuml baseline-computation
title US-04: Reference Route Setup - Static Routes Seeded at Startup

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

participant "Load\nGenerator" as LoadGen
database "TimescaleDB\nroute_references" as RouteRef
database "TimescaleDB\nroute_reference_points" as RoutePoints
participant "Deviation\nDetector" as Detector

note over LoadGen
  At startup, the synthetic load generator
  seeds known route definitions into TimescaleDB.
  Real ADS-B/AIS entities have no assigned route —
  they are skipped by the Deviation Detector.
end note

LoadGen -> RouteRef : insert route header\n{ route_id, entity_id, route_name,\n  corridor_threshold_metres, source }

LoadGen -> RoutePoints : insert ordered waypoints\n{ route_id, sequence_no, lat, lon }\n(one row per waypoint; pairs define route segments)

note over RoutePoints
  Waypoints define the expected corridor.
  corridor_threshold_metres lives on route_references.
  Seeded from the generator's known route definition —
  deterministic, injectable, consistent with
  the anomalies the generator will produce.
  No statistical averaging of historical positions.
end note

Detector -> RouteRef : look up entity in route_references
RouteRef --> Detector : row found (entity has an assigned reference route)

note over Detector
  If no row found: entity has no reference route.
  Skip without publishing to deviation.candidates.
  Real ADS-B/AIS entities fall through here in v1.
end note

Detector -> RoutePoints : fetch ordered waypoints for route_id
RoutePoints --> Detector : ordered waypoint list

@enduml
